diff --git a/README b/README
index 97d4bb4..02bf825 100644
--- a/README
+++ b/README
@@ -1046,6 +1046,29 @@ OBJECT-ORIENTED INTERFACE
 
         For the type argument see Cpanel::JSON::XS::Type.
 
+    $bytes_written = $json->encode_to ($filehandle, $perl_scalar,
+    $json_type)
+        Like "encode", but writes the JSON representation directly to
+        $filehandle as it is generated, instead of building the whole result
+        in memory and returning it as a string. Returns the number of bytes
+        (octets) written, and croaks (as "encode" does) if $perl_scalar
+        cannot be represented, and if the write to $filehandle fails.
+
+        This is useful for very large data structures, where it lowers peak
+        memory usage and gives the reader on the other end a head start
+        instead of waiting for the whole document to be assembled first:
+
+           open my $fh, ">", "big.json" or die $!;
+           $json->encode_to ($fh, $data);
+           close $fh;
+
+        "encode_to" always writes raw bytes: character data is emitted using
+        the same octet encoding "encode" would produce for the object's
+        current flags (see "utf8", "ascii", "latin1", "binary"), regardless
+        of any PerlIO encoding layer already present on $filehandle. As with
+        "encode", do not additionally push a :encoding(UTF-8) (or similar)
+        layer onto the handle, or the output will be double-encoded.
+
     $perl_scalar = $json->decode ($json_text, my $json_type)
         The opposite of "encode": expects a JSON text and tries to parse it,
         returning the resulting simple scalar or reference. Croaks on error.
